        'ai-in-recruitment-what-to-automate-what-stays-human' => [
            'title' => 'AI in Recruitment: What Should Be Automated — and What Should Stay Human?',
            'category' => 'AI & Recruitment',
            'excerpt' => 'AI can improve speed and signal extraction, but the strongest hiring systems are explicit about where automation ends and recruiter judgement begins.',
            'image' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552664730-d307ca884978?auto=format&fit=crop&q=82&w=1400',
            'read_time' => '6 min',
            'meta_description' => 'A practical framework for using AI in recruitment: what to automate in sourcing, parsing and workflow, and what should remain human-led.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>The useful question is not whether recruitment should use AI.</strong> It is where AI improves the process and where it can create false confidence.</p>
<h2>Good uses of AI: information processing</h2><p>AI is well suited to repetitive work such as CV parsing, skill extraction, search expansion, interview-note summarisation, candidate communication drafts, workflow reminders and structured comparison of evidence already collected.</p>
<h2>Good uses of AI: increasing recruiter coverage</h2><p>Recruiters can review more information, test broader talent pools and prepare more consistently when AI is used as decision support rather than as an automatic verdict.</p>
<h2>Where human judgement remains essential</h2><p>Motivation, credibility, leadership context, stakeholder fit, career trade-offs, communication nuance and the interpretation of achievements all require context. Senior hiring especially depends on understanding what happened around the candidate, not just what appears on a CV.</p>
<h2>Do not turn a matching score into a hiring decision</h2><p>Matching systems can be useful for prioritisation. They should not become a substitute for recruiter validation, particularly where CV wording, title conventions or non-linear careers can distort automated scoring.</p>
<h2>Automation should reduce friction, not remove accountability</h2><p>Automated scheduling, reminders and status communication are valuable. But important candidate conversations, rejections, compensation discussions and sensitive leadership outreach still need clear human ownership.</p>
<h2>Audit the input before trusting the output</h2><p>AI can only work with the information available to it. Poor job descriptions, inconsistent interview notes and vague success criteria will produce faster inconsistency rather than better hiring.</p>
<h2>A simple operating principle</h2><p><strong>Automate repeatable processing. Keep consequential judgement human-led.</strong> The point of AI in recruitment should be to give recruiters more time and better signal, not to hide responsibility behind a score.</p>
<p>See HiredNext’s approach to <a href="/about">human-led, technology-enabled hiring</a> and <a href="/hiring-intelligence">Hiring Intelligence</a>.</p>
